  "candidateVersion": "0.7.21",
  "method": "Windows loopback FTP/SFTP with automatic backups enabled and 3 ms added protocol delay. FTP uses exactly one configured connection; SFTP uses one authenticated SSH session. Six files: median of three paired runs; 24 files: one larger paired run. Background work is bounded separately from the FTP wire lane.",
  "upstream": "This compares the published 0.7.20 engine with the background-pipelined candidate. It is not a new upstream speed-parity measurement.",
  "limitations": "Local fixture results only; production latency, disk and permission-worker startup vary. The 24-file results are single runs. Remote permission commands still consume the serialized FTP lane. Git commit and verification still wait for all originals to finish, before any overwrite.",
  "proofs": [
    "Local empty staging/ACL preparation starts while remote read setup is pending; no bytes are written before ACL success.",
    "A later remote read starts while an earlier local backup close is deliberately held.",
    "Each real FTP connection rejects overlapping PASV/data operations in the wire regression; changing background concurrency reuses the same connection.",
    "Local background permission failure drains the wire queue, preserves server originals, and a retry succeeds on the same connection.",
    "Background initialization and preparation are drained before cleanup on failure.",
    "Git UI/CLI uploads use bounded consecutive-upload jobs; rename/delete barriers remain ordered, active jobs are drained on failure, and pending destructive work is skipped.",
    "Cancellation aborts active backup reads, skips queued FTP reads, drains their final responses and retains a usable primary connection."
  ],
